1
00:00:00,096 --> 00:00:01,282
You are the item the

2
00:00:01,282 --> 00:00:03,447
quartermaster left off his list.

3
00:00:03,885 --> 00:00:05,942
Last time, he told you what he took

4
00:00:05,942 --> 00:00:07,371
from the merchant sloop:

5
00:00:07,371 --> 00:00:13,141
flour, rum, rope, sailcloth and a few bolts of cloth.

6
00:00:13,141 --> 00:00:14,475
He never mentioned the

7
00:00:14,475 --> 00:00:17,036
passenger with the medicine chest.

8
00:00:17,036 --> 00:00:18,885
That passenger was you.

9
00:00:18,885 --> 00:00:21,040
Three episodes ago, you were Tom.

10
00:00:21,040 --> 00:00:22,513
Then you were the captain,

11
00:00:22,513 --> 00:00:24,400
and then the quartermaster.

12
00:00:24,823 --> 00:00:25,539
This time,

13
00:00:25,539 --> 00:00:27,884
you are not one of the crew at all.

14
00:00:27,884 --> 00:00:30,051
You are the ship's surgeon they took,

15
00:00:30,051 --> 00:00:32,976
and nobody asked whether you wanted the job.

16
00:00:32,976 --> 00:00:35,365
Imagine you are a surgeon of about forty,

17
00:00:35,365 --> 00:00:36,532
travelling as a

18
00:00:36,532 --> 00:00:39,137
passenger to a new post on another island,

19
00:00:39,137 --> 00:00:40,431
around seventeen seventeen.

20
00:00:40,431 --> 00:00:43,297
You wear a plain black coat

21
00:00:43,297 --> 00:00:44,611
over a white shirt,

22
00:00:44,611 --> 00:00:47,556
with a long leather apron, and your gray hair

23
00:00:47,556 --> 00:00:49,474
is tied back with a black ribbon.

24
00:00:49,474 --> 00:00:50,222
You,

25
00:00:50,222 --> 00:00:52,563
this crew and this voyage are imagined.

26
00:00:52,563 --> 00:00:54,933
The problems come from trial records,

27
00:00:54,933 --> 00:00:56,414
eyewitness accounts,

28
00:00:56,414 --> 00:01:00,693
archaeology and historians' research on real pirate crews.

29
00:01:01,131 --> 00:01:02,425
And remember what the

30
00:01:02,425 --> 00:01:03,777
crew did for a living.

31
00:01:03,777 --> 00:01:05,722
Every wound you will treat

32
00:01:05,722 --> 00:01:07,103
was earned robbing someone.

33
00:01:07,103 --> 00:01:08,581
The sailors on the ships they

34
00:01:08,581 --> 00:01:09,971
board are not scenery,

35
00:01:09,971 --> 00:01:13,420
even when our story keeps the violence off screen.

36
00:01:13,420 --> 00:01:15,344
Your capture takes about a minute.

37
00:01:15,344 --> 00:01:17,860
The quartermaster opens your wooden chest,

38
00:01:17,860 --> 00:01:19,005
looking for silver.

39
00:01:19,005 --> 00:01:20,136
He finds jars,

40
00:01:20,136 --> 00:01:23,186
bandages, a small saw and a row of knives,

41
00:01:23,186 --> 00:01:25,271
and he stops looking for silver.

42
00:01:25,271 --> 00:01:27,183
The captain says the one word

43
00:01:27,183 --> 00:01:29,797
you were hoping not to hear: keep.

44
00:01:29,797 --> 00:01:31,971
Real crews did exactly this.

45
00:01:31,971 --> 00:01:34,426
Pirates held on to captured

46
00:01:34,426 --> 00:01:36,320
men whose skills they needed,

47
00:01:36,320 --> 00:01:38,656
and surgeons were high on that list.

48
00:01:38,656 --> 00:01:39,786
In seventeen nineteen,

49
00:01:39,786 --> 00:01:42,243
at the Sierra Leone River in West Africa,

50
00:01:42,243 --> 00:01:45,933
pirates captured the slave-trading captain William Snelgrave.

51
00:01:45,933 --> 00:01:47,778
He later wrote that they intended to

52
00:01:47,778 --> 00:01:50,896
take his surgeon, James Bleau, by force.

53
00:01:50,896 --> 00:01:52,591
Snelgrave pleaded for him.

54
00:01:52,591 --> 00:01:54,396
Bleau, he told them,

55
00:01:54,396 --> 00:01:57,209
grieved so much that if he did not die quickly,

56
00:01:57,209 --> 00:01:59,782
he would be of no use to them anyway.

57
00:01:59,782 --> 00:02:01,295
It did not work.

58
00:02:01,637 --> 00:02:02,997
Bleau was set free only because

59
00:02:02,997 --> 00:02:04,269
the surgeon of a French ship

60
00:02:04,269 --> 00:02:06,525
joined the pirates that same evening.

61
00:02:06,525 --> 00:02:07,798
The crew needed a surgeon.

62
00:02:07,798 --> 00:02:09,574
It did not much care which one.

63
00:02:09,574 --> 00:02:12,181
Blackbeard's crew did the same.

64
00:02:12,181 --> 00:02:14,162
In November seventeen seventeen,

65
00:02:14,162 --> 00:02:16,965
they captured the French slave ship La Concorde.

66
00:02:16,965 --> 00:02:19,297
The Queen Anne's Revenge project,

67
00:02:19,297 --> 00:02:20,660
which excavates her wreck,

68
00:02:20,660 --> 00:02:23,752
writes that ten Frenchmen were forced to join the pirates,

69
00:02:23,752 --> 00:02:26,728
among them surgeons, carpenters and a cook.

70
00:02:26,728 --> 00:02:28,866
The archaeologist Linda

71
00:02:28,866 --> 00:02:30,545
Carnes-McNaughton says all three of the

72
00:02:30,545 --> 00:02:32,571
ship's surgeons were forced to stay.

73
00:02:32,882 --> 00:02:34,171
The surgeons were not the only

74
00:02:34,171 --> 00:02:35,801
people kept against their will.

75
00:02:36,256 --> 00:02:37,897
The Smithsonian says the

76
00:02:37,897 --> 00:02:40,014
pirates kept sixty-one of the Africans

77
00:02:40,014 --> 00:02:42,024
held captive aboard La Concorde.

78
00:02:42,024 --> 00:02:44,046
The Queen Anne's Revenge

79
00:02:44,046 --> 00:02:45,415
project says about seventy-five,

80
00:02:45,415 --> 00:02:47,442
and notes that sources vary.

81
00:02:47,752 --> 00:02:49,295
Most of the others were handed

82
00:02:49,295 --> 00:02:51,536
back to the French captain, to be sold.

83
00:02:51,990 --> 00:02:54,176
Nobody asked those people anything,

84
00:02:54,176 --> 00:02:55,674
and no court would ever

85
00:02:55,674 --> 00:02:57,797
ask how they had come aboard.

86
00:02:57,797 --> 00:03:00,157
Before your merchant sloop sails away,

87
00:03:00,499 --> 00:03:01,921
you ask her frightened

88
00:03:01,921 --> 00:03:03,899
young sailor to tell anyone who

89
00:03:03,899 --> 00:03:06,943
asks that you were taken by force.

90
00:03:06,943 --> 00:03:08,791
He nods so hard you are

91
00:03:08,791 --> 00:03:10,486
not sure he heard a word.

92
00:03:10,486 --> 00:03:12,485
Tom carries your chest

93
00:03:12,485 --> 00:03:14,001
across to the pirate sloop.

94
00:03:14,001 --> 00:03:15,625
He carries it very carefully,

95
00:03:15,625 --> 00:03:18,914
the way people carry anything that contains knives.

96
00:03:18,914 --> 00:03:19,664
That evening,

97
00:03:19,664 --> 00:03:22,984
the quartermaster brings out the articles and a pen.

98
00:03:22,984 --> 00:03:24,621
You read every clause,

99
00:03:24,621 --> 00:03:26,939
looking for the one that sends surgeons home.

100
00:03:26,939 --> 00:03:28,096
There isn't one.

101
00:03:28,469 --> 00:03:30,353
Whether a captured surgeon

102
00:03:30,353 --> 00:03:32,446
signed mattered a great deal.

103
00:03:32,446 --> 00:03:33,620
In seventeen twenty-two,

104
00:03:33,620 --> 00:03:37,466
Bartholomew Roberts's captured crews were tried at Cape Coast Castle,

105
00:03:37,466 --> 00:03:39,296
on the West African coast.

106
00:03:39,296 --> 00:03:42,424
A witness told the court that one surgeon,

107
00:03:42,424 --> 00:03:44,551
Peter Scudamore, had signed the pirates'

108
00:03:44,551 --> 00:03:47,100
articles "with a great deal of alacrity,"

109
00:03:47,100 --> 00:03:49,505
and was the first surgeon who had done so.

110
00:03:49,505 --> 00:03:51,786
Our old friend Captain Charles

111
00:03:51,786 --> 00:03:54,232
Johnson prints his own version of that

112
00:03:54,232 --> 00:03:56,822
trial in his General History of the Pyrates,

113
00:03:56,822 --> 00:03:58,071
from seventeen twenty-four.

114
00:03:58,071 --> 00:04:00,428
Johnson is a pen name,

115
00:04:00,428 --> 00:04:03,354
and his book mixes fact with invention.

116
00:04:03,713 --> 00:04:05,113
His version adds that,

117
00:04:05,113 --> 00:04:07,486
before Scudamore, the crew had changed

118
00:04:07,486 --> 00:04:09,521
surgeons after they served a while,

119
00:04:09,521 --> 00:04:11,680
and never obliged them to sign.

120
00:04:11,680 --> 00:04:12,617
Another surgeon,

121
00:04:12,617 --> 00:04:15,376
George Wilson, had been a passenger like

122
00:04:15,376 --> 00:04:17,057
you when pirates took his ship.

123
00:04:17,057 --> 00:04:18,371
You will meet him again.

124
00:04:18,371 --> 00:04:20,422
He asked a witness at his own trial to

125
00:04:20,422 --> 00:04:22,412
confirm that he had challenged Roberts

126
00:04:22,412 --> 00:04:24,905
over why surgeons were now made to sign.

127
00:04:24,905 --> 00:04:26,857
The witness said yes.

128
00:04:26,857 --> 00:04:28,655
It was not a small question.

129
00:04:28,655 --> 00:04:29,956
A signature turned a

130
00:04:29,956 --> 00:04:32,059
prisoner into a member.

131
00:04:32,059 --> 00:04:34,183
So you do not sign.

132
00:04:34,781 --> 00:04:35,730
The captain shrugs.

133
00:04:35,730 --> 00:04:36,583
The quartermaster

134
00:04:36,583 --> 00:04:38,306
writes something in his book.

135
00:04:38,631 --> 00:04:41,308
You very much hope the word is "forced."

136
00:04:41,308 --> 00:04:43,782
Signed or not, you will be paid.

137
00:04:43,782 --> 00:04:47,294
In the articles Johnson printed for Roberts's crew,

138
00:04:47,294 --> 00:04:48,156
the master,

139
00:04:48,156 --> 00:04:52,282
boatswain and gunner received one and a half shares of a prize,

140
00:04:52,282 --> 00:04:54,004
and other officers one and a quarter.

141
00:04:54,435 --> 00:04:55,881
The trial record shows that

142
00:04:55,881 --> 00:04:58,466
Roberts's men even voted for a chief surgeon.

143
00:04:58,466 --> 00:05:00,663
Scudamore had been elected "in

144
00:05:00,663 --> 00:05:03,296
opposition to" two other surgeons.

145
00:05:03,296 --> 00:05:04,756
One of those rivals,

146
00:05:04,756 --> 00:05:07,860
Adam Comrie, said George Wilson had told him he

147
00:05:07,860 --> 00:05:09,161
hoped Comrie would win it,

148
00:05:09,161 --> 00:05:12,242
because "a quarter share, which they had more than others,

149
00:05:12,242 --> 00:05:14,051
would be worth looking after."

150
00:05:14,051 --> 00:05:17,038
On your sloop, there is no election.

151
00:05:17,476 --> 00:05:19,094
You are the only candidate,

152
00:05:19,094 --> 00:05:20,192
and you never ran.

153
00:05:20,192 --> 00:05:22,844
You receive a quarter share more than Tom.

154
00:05:22,844 --> 00:05:24,811
You are a prisoner with a pay rise.

155
00:05:25,328 --> 00:05:27,251
The crew does not admire you.

156
00:05:27,251 --> 00:05:29,025
It admires your chest.

157
00:05:29,334 --> 00:05:30,841
They have good reason.

158
00:05:30,841 --> 00:05:32,699
A generation earlier,

159
00:05:32,699 --> 00:05:33,946
Alexander Exquemelin,

160
00:05:33,946 --> 00:05:37,122
who served the buccaneers as a barber-surgeon,

161
00:05:37,122 --> 00:05:39,701
described their agreements in a book first

162
00:05:39,701 --> 00:05:41,122
published in sixteen seventy-eight.

163
00:05:41,609 --> 00:05:43,348
Before any prize was divided,

164
00:05:43,348 --> 00:05:45,698
he wrote, the surgeon received a salary

165
00:05:45,698 --> 00:05:48,570
for himself and his chest of medicines,

166
00:05:48,570 --> 00:05:50,991
usually rated at two hundred or

167
00:05:50,991 --> 00:05:52,835
two hundred and fifty pieces of eight.

168
00:05:52,835 --> 00:05:53,838
The carpenter,

169
00:05:53,838 --> 00:05:57,836
who repaired and fitted out the whole ship,

170
00:05:57,836 --> 00:05:59,752
got one hundred or one hundred and fifty.

171
00:06:00,137 --> 00:06:01,629
A box of jars was rated higher

172
00:06:01,629 --> 00:06:03,806
than the man who kept the ship afloat.

173
00:06:04,212 --> 00:06:05,392
Medicine could even be

174
00:06:05,392 --> 00:06:06,871
worth a town's terror.

175
00:06:07,437 --> 00:06:08,490
In May seventeen eighteen,

176
00:06:08,490 --> 00:06:12,548
Blackbeard's squadron blockaded the harbor of Charleston,

177
00:06:12,548 --> 00:06:13,430
South Carolina,

178
00:06:13,430 --> 00:06:17,390
for about a week, seizing ships and taking hostages.

179
00:06:17,390 --> 00:06:19,505
The South Carolina Encyclopedia

180
00:06:19,505 --> 00:06:21,267
names one of them:

181
00:06:21,267 --> 00:06:24,572
Samuel Wragg, a member of the colony's council.

182
00:06:25,573 --> 00:06:29,382
Royal Museums Greenwich says Blackbeard

183
00:06:29,382 --> 00:06:31,654
demanded a ransom that included a chest

184
00:06:31,654 --> 00:06:32,961
of expensive medicines,

185
00:06:32,961 --> 00:06:36,512
and threatened to kill his prisoners if he did not get it.

186
00:06:36,512 --> 00:06:37,980
The Smithsonian calls the

187
00:06:37,980 --> 00:06:40,482
medicine chest his most urgent demand.

188
00:06:40,999 --> 00:06:42,277
The governor gave in.

189
00:06:42,277 --> 00:06:43,439
The chest went out,

190
00:06:43,439 --> 00:06:47,296
the prisoners were released, and the squadron sailed away.

191
00:06:47,296 --> 00:06:50,126
Johnson's book alone puts the chest's

192
00:06:50,126 --> 00:06:52,563
value at three to four hundred pounds.

193
00:06:53,097 --> 00:06:54,196
Think about that.

194
00:06:54,196 --> 00:06:56,089
A pirate captain sat outside a

195
00:06:56,089 --> 00:06:58,391
colonial port with a powerful squadron,

196
00:06:58,391 --> 00:07:00,816
and what he wanted first was medicine.

197
00:07:01,349 --> 00:07:03,508
Whatever his crew was suffering from,

198
00:07:03,508 --> 00:07:05,428
cutlasses were not curing it.

199
00:07:05,428 --> 00:07:05,959
Usually,

200
00:07:05,959 --> 00:07:09,733
pirates simply took medicine from the ships they robbed.

201
00:07:09,733 --> 00:07:11,350
At the Cape Coast trial,

202
00:07:11,350 --> 00:07:14,780
Captain Joseph Trahern of the King Solomon said he

203
00:07:14,780 --> 00:07:18,603
watched Scudamore carry off Trahern's own backgammon tables,

204
00:07:18,603 --> 00:07:21,240
his surgeon's main instruments,

205
00:07:21,240 --> 00:07:24,267
some medicines, a quilt and a bolster.

206
00:07:24,689 --> 00:07:26,805
Scudamore answered that he had plundered

207
00:07:26,805 --> 00:07:28,832
medicine chests on orders from the

208
00:07:28,832 --> 00:07:31,503
company and from the chief surgeon of the day.

209
00:07:32,134 --> 00:07:33,902
Johnson's version adds that,

210
00:07:33,902 --> 00:07:37,355
by the pirates' own laws, the chief surgeon always

211
00:07:37,355 --> 00:07:39,303
gave the orders in that department,

212
00:07:39,303 --> 00:07:41,825
and that another surgeon had carried off a

213
00:07:41,825 --> 00:07:44,022
whole French medicine chest the same way.

214
00:07:44,428 --> 00:07:45,563
Pirate surgeons,

215
00:07:45,563 --> 00:07:47,767
it turns out, were also pirates.

216
00:07:47,767 --> 00:07:50,041
They simply had their own department.

217
00:07:50,041 --> 00:07:52,881
We know what a pirate surgeon worked with,

218
00:07:52,881 --> 00:07:53,855
because one surgeon's

219
00:07:53,855 --> 00:07:55,705
kit went to the bottom of the sea.

220
00:07:56,144 --> 00:07:57,481
In June seventeen eighteen,

221
00:07:57,481 --> 00:07:59,999
Blackbeard's flagship,

222
00:07:59,999 --> 00:08:03,452
the Queen Anne's Revenge, which had been La Concorde,

223
00:08:03,452 --> 00:08:06,322
ran aground at Beaufort Inlet, North Carolina.

224
00:08:06,680 --> 00:08:07,666
Centuries later,

225
00:08:07,666 --> 00:08:11,587
archaeologists began bringing her back up, piece by piece.

226
00:08:11,587 --> 00:08:12,574
Among the finds,

227
00:08:12,574 --> 00:08:16,440
Carnes-McNaughton identified a surgeon's working tools:

228
00:08:16,440 --> 00:08:18,711
a brass mortar and pestle,

229
00:08:18,711 --> 00:08:21,659
two sets of nesting weights for measuring out doses,

230
00:08:21,659 --> 00:08:24,502
a silver needle and the remains of scissors.

231
00:08:24,502 --> 00:08:25,882
There were two pump clysters,

232
00:08:25,882 --> 00:08:27,251
used to give enemas.

233
00:08:27,251 --> 00:08:28,788
There was a porringer,

234
00:08:28,788 --> 00:08:32,312
a small bowl that may have been used for bloodletting.

235
00:08:32,312 --> 00:08:33,490
And two pairs of brass set

236
00:08:33,490 --> 00:08:36,103
screws may have belonged to a tourniquet,

237
00:08:36,103 --> 00:08:37,566
used during amputations.

238
00:08:37,566 --> 00:08:39,504
Then there is the syringe.

239
00:08:39,877 --> 00:08:41,950
It is a pewter urethral syringe,

240
00:08:41,950 --> 00:08:43,332
and chemical analysis

241
00:08:43,332 --> 00:08:46,029
found traces of mercury inside it.

242
00:08:46,029 --> 00:08:47,348
In the pirates' day,

243
00:08:47,348 --> 00:08:49,944
mercury was a treatment for syphilis.

244
00:08:49,944 --> 00:08:52,360
"Eventually the mercury kills you,"

245
00:08:52,360 --> 00:08:55,003
Carnes-McNaughton told Live Science.

246
00:08:55,328 --> 00:08:57,218
That was the medicine of the time:

247
00:08:57,218 --> 00:08:59,575
a cure that could poison the patient,

248
00:08:59,575 --> 00:09:02,166
given by a man who had been kidnapped to give it.

249
00:09:02,636 --> 00:09:04,969
Remember who was aboard that ship.

250
00:09:04,969 --> 00:09:06,501
Three surgeons who had never

251
00:09:06,501 --> 00:09:07,550
asked to be pirates,

252
00:09:07,550 --> 00:09:09,698
treating the men who had taken them.

253
00:09:10,023 --> 00:09:12,645
So when your own crew calls it "our" chest,

254
00:09:12,645 --> 00:09:14,506
you know whose chest it was.

255
00:09:14,506 --> 00:09:16,281
The quartermaster has already

256
00:09:16,281 --> 00:09:18,497
written yours into the common stock,

257
00:09:18,497 --> 00:09:20,376
between the rope and the rum.

258
00:09:20,910 --> 00:09:22,609
Inside it are a mortar,

259
00:09:22,609 --> 00:09:26,576
a few jars, bandages, a saw you hope to keep wrapped,

260
00:09:26,576 --> 00:09:28,010
and a bottle of mercury

261
00:09:28,010 --> 00:09:30,450
you trust a little less every day.

262
00:09:30,450 --> 00:09:33,245
Your first patient is the crewmate.

263
00:09:33,245 --> 00:09:35,837
A rope ran through his hand while he was hauling,

264
00:09:35,837 --> 00:09:37,914
and the palm is badly burned.

265
00:09:37,914 --> 00:09:39,279
It is not a battle wound,

266
00:09:39,279 --> 00:09:42,060
but injuries like it ended careers at sea.

267
00:09:42,060 --> 00:09:43,294
You clean it,

268
00:09:43,667 --> 00:09:46,260
bandage it, and tell him to keep it dry.

269
00:09:46,260 --> 00:09:47,256
On a ship.

270
00:09:47,598 --> 00:09:49,471
Then he asks what it is worth.

271
00:09:49,471 --> 00:09:51,482
Pirate articles answered

272
00:09:51,482 --> 00:09:53,353
that question with a price list.

273
00:09:53,353 --> 00:09:54,657
In Roberts's articles,

274
00:09:54,657 --> 00:09:57,476
as Johnson printed them, any man who lost a

275
00:09:57,476 --> 00:09:59,849
limb or became a cripple in the

276
00:09:59,849 --> 00:10:02,112
company's service received eight hundred

277
00:10:02,112 --> 00:10:03,897
dollars out of the public stock,

278
00:10:03,897 --> 00:10:06,377
"and for lesser hurts, proportionably."

279
00:10:06,702 --> 00:10:09,857
Exquemelin's buccaneers were more exact.

280
00:10:09,857 --> 00:10:11,118
Six hundred pieces of

281
00:10:11,118 --> 00:10:12,807
eight for a right arm.

282
00:10:12,807 --> 00:10:13,696
Five hundred for a

283
00:10:13,696 --> 00:10:16,577
left arm or a right leg.

284
00:10:16,577 --> 00:10:18,514
Four hundred for a left leg.

285
00:10:18,514 --> 00:10:19,733
One hundred for an eye,

286
00:10:19,733 --> 00:10:21,497
and the same for a finger.

287
00:10:21,497 --> 00:10:23,303
The economist Peter Leeson

288
00:10:23,303 --> 00:10:25,114
calls this social insurance.

289
00:10:25,114 --> 00:10:27,155
The money came out of the common

290
00:10:27,155 --> 00:10:29,248
stock before any shares were counted,

291
00:10:29,248 --> 00:10:31,014
and the articles even priced a

292
00:10:31,014 --> 00:10:33,552
right arm differently from a left.

293
00:10:33,552 --> 00:10:35,287
The same buccaneer agreements

294
00:10:35,287 --> 00:10:36,963
offered a second way to pay:

295
00:10:36,963 --> 00:10:40,603
six enslaved people for a right arm, five for a left.

296
00:10:40,603 --> 00:10:43,022
That line is not a curiosity.

297
00:10:43,022 --> 00:10:44,680
It shows exactly whom the

298
00:10:44,680 --> 00:10:46,506
buccaneers counted as people,

299
00:10:46,506 --> 00:10:48,568
and whom they counted as payment.

300
00:10:48,568 --> 00:10:50,261
Back on your sloop,

301
00:10:50,261 --> 00:10:53,206
you discover the part of the job nobody mentioned.

302
00:10:53,206 --> 00:10:54,644
Somebody has to say

303
00:10:54,644 --> 00:10:57,223
how bad a wound really is.

304
00:10:57,836 --> 00:10:59,140
The whole crew turns and looks

305
00:10:59,140 --> 00:11:00,829
at the man in the leather apron.

306
00:11:01,267 --> 00:11:02,558
You tell the crewmate his

307
00:11:02,558 --> 00:11:04,199
hand will heal completely.

308
00:11:04,654 --> 00:11:06,655
He takes it as a personal insult.

309
00:11:07,286 --> 00:11:07,706
Eight hundred dollars

310
00:11:07,706 --> 00:11:10,443
was a much better diagnosis.

311
00:11:10,913 --> 00:11:11,627
Tom asks,

312
00:11:11,627 --> 00:11:14,712
very casually, what a finger is worth.

313
00:11:15,166 --> 00:11:17,079
You tell him to keep all ten.

314
00:11:17,079 --> 00:11:19,277
A few days later, a sail appears.

315
00:11:19,277 --> 00:11:22,148
The captain takes command for the chase,

316
00:11:22,148 --> 00:11:24,017
the quartermaster checks the boat,

317
00:11:24,017 --> 00:11:26,895
and somebody sends you below with your chest.

318
00:11:26,895 --> 00:11:28,319
Nobody explains why.

319
00:11:28,319 --> 00:11:30,042
Then you understand.

320
00:11:30,578 --> 00:11:32,138
If anything goes wrong up there,

321
00:11:32,138 --> 00:11:34,188
it will come down the ladder to you.

322
00:11:34,659 --> 00:11:36,803
You lay out bandages on a sea chest by

323
00:11:36,803 --> 00:11:39,381
lantern light and listen to the feet above.

324
00:11:39,381 --> 00:11:40,492
The merchant ship

325
00:11:40,492 --> 00:11:42,683
surrenders without a shot.

326
00:11:42,683 --> 00:11:44,628
Nobody comes down the ladder.

327
00:11:44,628 --> 00:11:46,425
You have never been

328
00:11:46,425 --> 00:11:47,987
so happy to be useless.

329
00:11:47,987 --> 00:11:51,553
Not every pirate surgeon was a reluctant gentleman,

330
00:11:51,553 --> 00:11:53,355
and not every one was sober.

331
00:11:53,857 --> 00:11:55,342
Johnson's book alone tells

332
00:11:55,342 --> 00:11:57,622
this story about Edward Low's crew.

333
00:11:58,109 --> 00:11:59,360
Low was cut on the jaw by

334
00:11:59,360 --> 00:12:01,414
one of his own men's cutlasses.

335
00:12:01,414 --> 00:12:03,253
The surgeon stitched the wound while,

336
00:12:03,253 --> 00:12:04,702
as Johnson puts it,

337
00:12:04,702 --> 00:12:08,291
"tolerably drunk, as it was customary for everybody to be."

338
00:12:08,617 --> 00:12:10,256
When Low complained about the work,

339
00:12:10,256 --> 00:12:11,394
the surgeon punched him,

340
00:12:11,394 --> 00:12:14,947
broke open the stitches, and told him to sew it up himself.

341
00:12:14,947 --> 00:12:16,940
True or not, the story shows how

342
00:12:16,940 --> 00:12:19,447
readers pictured a pirate surgeon.

343
00:12:19,447 --> 00:12:20,930
You decide to stay sober,

344
00:12:20,930 --> 00:12:23,448
which makes you the strangest man aboard.

345
00:12:23,448 --> 00:12:25,571
George Wilson's defence at Cape

346
00:12:25,571 --> 00:12:27,837
Coast was strange in its own way.

347
00:12:27,837 --> 00:12:29,547
He asked a witness to confirm that

348
00:12:29,547 --> 00:12:31,764
he had called the pirates' firing

349
00:12:31,764 --> 00:12:32,884
at Whydah good sport,

350
00:12:32,884 --> 00:12:35,372
"were it not in an unlawful way," meaning the

351
00:12:35,372 --> 00:12:38,018
practice it gave him in his business.

352
00:12:38,018 --> 00:12:39,504
The witness said yes.

353
00:12:39,504 --> 00:12:40,989
That was his defence.

354
00:12:40,989 --> 00:12:43,760
Wilson's story was tangled from the start.

355
00:12:43,760 --> 00:12:45,424
Roberts's crew had already

356
00:12:45,424 --> 00:12:47,606
taken him once, and he ended up

357
00:12:47,606 --> 00:12:49,968
stranded on the West African coast.

358
00:12:49,968 --> 00:12:52,465
At Sestos, the master of the Elizabeth,

359
00:12:52,465 --> 00:12:53,214
John Sharp,

360
00:12:53,214 --> 00:12:56,135
paid local people a ransom of three pounds five

361
00:12:56,135 --> 00:12:58,443
shillings in goods to free him,

362
00:12:58,443 --> 00:13:01,377
and took Wilson's written promise to repay it.

363
00:13:01,377 --> 00:13:03,713
Then the pirates took the Elizabeth,

364
00:13:03,713 --> 00:13:06,345
with Wilson aboard as a passenger.

365
00:13:06,345 --> 00:13:08,267
When Sharp asked about the note,

366
00:13:08,267 --> 00:13:10,295
Wilson told him it did not matter,

367
00:13:10,295 --> 00:13:11,880
because he believed he would

368
00:13:11,880 --> 00:13:14,262
never come to England to pay it.

369
00:13:14,262 --> 00:13:16,548
Sharp repeated that remark in court.

370
00:13:16,548 --> 00:13:18,316
It did not sound like a

371
00:13:18,316 --> 00:13:20,165
man planning to go home.

372
00:13:20,165 --> 00:13:22,061
And Peter Scudamore,

373
00:13:22,061 --> 00:13:25,856
the surgeon who signed so eagerly, was not finished.

374
00:13:25,856 --> 00:13:28,502
After Roberts's ships were taken,

375
00:13:28,502 --> 00:13:30,643
he was left aboard Roberts's captured

376
00:13:30,643 --> 00:13:32,905
flagship as she sailed for Cape Coast,

377
00:13:32,905 --> 00:13:35,160
and a witness said he was treated not

378
00:13:35,160 --> 00:13:38,224
like a prisoner, but "with all humanity."

379
00:13:38,224 --> 00:13:41,245
He used that freedom to plan an escape.

380
00:13:41,245 --> 00:13:43,191
According to the trial record,

381
00:13:43,191 --> 00:13:45,074
he urged a fellow prisoner to join a

382
00:13:45,074 --> 00:13:47,581
rising with the Black men held aboard,

383
00:13:47,581 --> 00:13:49,349
take the ship, and sail down the

384
00:13:49,349 --> 00:13:51,513
coast to raise a new pirate company.

385
00:13:51,513 --> 00:13:54,314
He boasted that he knew how to navigate.

386
00:13:54,314 --> 00:13:56,774
The fellow prisoner was William Child,

387
00:13:56,774 --> 00:13:59,018
another surgeon, who had just been acquitted.

388
00:13:59,375 --> 00:14:00,765
Child reported the plan

389
00:14:00,765 --> 00:14:02,329
to the officer in command.

390
00:14:02,329 --> 00:14:04,234
So among Roberts's surgeons,

391
00:14:04,234 --> 00:14:06,280
one signed with enthusiasm,

392
00:14:06,280 --> 00:14:10,007
one protested about signing, and one turned in another.

393
00:14:10,007 --> 00:14:10,932
Your profession,

394
00:14:10,932 --> 00:14:14,006
it seems, held every kind of man that the crew did.

395
00:14:14,460 --> 00:14:16,887
Courts cared about how a man came aboard.

396
00:14:16,887 --> 00:14:18,601
They cared even more about

397
00:14:18,601 --> 00:14:20,374
what he did once he was there.

398
00:14:20,374 --> 00:14:22,595
Johnson's version of the Cape Coast

399
00:14:22,595 --> 00:14:24,812
trial puts the court's view bluntly:

400
00:14:24,812 --> 00:14:28,412
"it is not so material how a man comes among pirates,

401
00:14:28,412 --> 00:14:30,817
as how he acts when he is there."

402
00:14:30,817 --> 00:14:33,356
That sentence keeps you awake.

403
00:14:33,356 --> 00:14:34,332
You never signed.

404
00:14:34,332 --> 00:14:37,163
But you have bandaged the crewmate's hand,

405
00:14:37,163 --> 00:14:38,465
dressed Tom's blistered

406
00:14:38,465 --> 00:14:40,828
palms and dosed the captain's fever.

407
00:14:41,154 --> 00:14:43,683
Every bandage makes the crew stronger.

408
00:14:43,683 --> 00:14:46,166
A prosecutor could call that help.

409
00:14:46,166 --> 00:14:48,487
Scudamore's actions spoke against him:

410
00:14:48,862 --> 00:14:50,363
the eager signature,

411
00:14:50,363 --> 00:14:52,951
the plundered chests, the plot.

412
00:14:53,357 --> 00:14:54,851
He was sentenced to death

413
00:14:54,851 --> 00:14:56,748
and hanged at Cape Coast Castle.

414
00:14:56,748 --> 00:14:58,174
Child was acquitted.

415
00:14:58,174 --> 00:14:58,756
In all,

416
00:14:58,756 --> 00:15:01,402
fifty-two of Roberts's men were hanged,

417
00:15:01,402 --> 00:15:03,260
and more than seventy were acquitted,

418
00:15:03,260 --> 00:15:06,272
most of them men who had been forced aboard.

419
00:15:06,272 --> 00:15:07,982
Then the dealer brings the news

420
00:15:07,982 --> 00:15:09,211
you have heard before:

421
00:15:09,211 --> 00:15:11,479
the king's pardon of seventeen seventeen.

422
00:15:11,479 --> 00:15:15,748
Surrender to a governor by the fifth of September,

423
00:15:15,748 --> 00:15:16,256
seventeen eighteen,

424
00:15:16,256 --> 00:15:19,192
and earlier piracies would be forgiven.

425
00:15:19,192 --> 00:15:20,316
You are not sure a

426
00:15:20,316 --> 00:15:22,214
forced man needs a pardon.

427
00:15:22,699 --> 00:15:23,991
You are also not sure a court

428
00:15:23,991 --> 00:15:25,755
would agree that you were forced.

429
00:15:26,144 --> 00:15:28,652
A pardon seems cheaper than an argument.

430
00:15:28,652 --> 00:15:30,984
You read the reward list, too.

431
00:15:30,984 --> 00:15:32,957
As the London Gazette printed it,

432
00:15:32,957 --> 00:15:35,412
a pirate commander was worth one hundred pounds,

433
00:15:35,412 --> 00:15:37,393
and every lieutenant,

434
00:15:37,393 --> 00:15:41,458
master, boatswain, carpenter and gunner forty.

435
00:15:41,944 --> 00:15:44,179
Surgeons are not named at all.

436
00:15:44,179 --> 00:15:44,828
Neither,

437
00:15:44,828 --> 00:15:47,438
you remember, were quartermasters.

438
00:15:47,438 --> 00:15:48,453
The king's clerks

439
00:15:48,453 --> 00:15:50,741
clearly never needed stitches.

440
00:15:50,741 --> 00:15:52,072
The crew votes on it.

441
00:15:52,072 --> 00:15:54,508
You never signed, so you have no vote.

442
00:15:54,508 --> 00:15:56,977
You hear the result with everyone else:

443
00:15:57,335 --> 00:15:59,446
the pardon wins, narrowly.

444
00:15:59,446 --> 00:16:02,837
The quartermaster divides the common chest,

445
00:16:02,837 --> 00:16:04,331
share by share.

446
00:16:05,107 --> 00:16:07,357
Then he hands you back your medicine chest,

447
00:16:07,357 --> 00:16:08,731
lighter than before.

448
00:16:09,522 --> 00:16:11,882
The silver needle stays behind.

449
00:16:11,882 --> 00:16:13,472
Gold and silver,

450
00:16:13,472 --> 00:16:16,212
he reminds you, are not returnable.

451
00:16:16,212 --> 00:16:18,815
Your crew voted on that long ago.

452
00:16:18,815 --> 00:16:21,305
Tom carries the chest ashore for you,

453
00:16:21,305 --> 00:16:24,199
as carefully as on the day you came aboard.

454
00:16:24,684 --> 00:16:27,091
The crewmate shows you his healed hand.

455
00:16:27,091 --> 00:16:29,171
He is still slightly offended.

456
00:16:29,171 --> 00:16:31,046
So why was being a pirate

457
00:16:31,046 --> 00:16:33,242
surgeon worse than you think?

458
00:16:33,242 --> 00:16:34,486
Because the crew wanted your

459
00:16:34,486 --> 00:16:36,201
chest more than they wanted you.

460
00:16:36,639 --> 00:16:39,003
Because you were a prisoner with a pay rise,

461
00:16:39,003 --> 00:16:41,369
and your best medicine was a poison.

462
00:16:41,369 --> 00:16:42,786
Because your patients

463
00:16:42,786 --> 00:16:45,146
wanted a price list, not a cure.

464
00:16:45,146 --> 00:16:48,025
And because every wound you closed

465
00:16:48,025 --> 00:16:50,097
helped a crew that robbed other people.

466
00:16:50,097 --> 00:16:51,237
A court would not ask

467
00:16:51,237 --> 00:16:52,883
how you came among pirates.

468
00:16:52,883 --> 00:16:55,182
It would ask what you did there.

469
00:16:55,734 --> 00:16:58,151
If you want another life behind the legends,

470
00:16:58,151 --> 00:16:59,811
and another job that

471
00:16:59,811 --> 00:17:02,092
sounds better than it was, subscribe.

472
00:17:02,092 --> 00:17:03,713
This is Pirate Pages:

473
00:17:04,071 --> 00:17:05,981
the lives behind the legends.
